WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:07.040
McElligott: The title of your talk is
"Time Poverty Amidst Digital Abundance."

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.920
Is time poverty mostly a perceptual thing?

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.320
I don't know how much that is actually a term of art

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.920
or just a coinage of yours?

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:24.160
Is it more, would you say, a trick
we are playing on ourselves?

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.210
Or is it also different populations

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.240
that are having different challenges?

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.800
Because certainly the person who is working three jobs

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.200
has a different kind of time poverty

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.540
than the person who just has that calendar.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.880
Wajcman: Well, here particularly what's very strong
is the kind of culture of busyness.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.430
I was lucky enough to do a radio interview

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.220
with William Gibson once on BBC radio

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.100
when his most recent book had come out.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.800
It was a couple of years ago
--you know, the science fiction writer.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.680
And he said to me: saying you're incredibly busy now

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.460
is just saying you're incredibly rich.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.580
That's the kind of equivalent now.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:05.640
And I've had people say to me at some of my talks...

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.980
a colleague of mine in Cambridge actually, 
who I've known for years said to me...

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.860
if I go back to my office now and there are no emails

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.380
I'll feel like a loser. I feel like a failure.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:19.680
But this is so much a measure of kind of our sense of

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.160
self and who we are and our importance and our status.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.680
That it's very hard to deconstruct.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.100
It's that culture, actually.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.640
I have to say that when I first came to America,

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.140
like 30 years ago,

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.560
my first job was in Edinburgh,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.920
various people said to me, you know

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.689
when you get to America all those American academics

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:47.730
they're all gonna tell you that they're
incredibly busy.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.180
You've got to be prepared for this.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:56.100
It is just part of what's culturally valued, I think.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.079
And that I think makes leisure

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:04.500
such an undervalued activity.

